          "type": "text",
          "text": "Good instinct — guess before I answer. And there's a subtlety in here worth pinning down: whether the second fold stacks the paper on itself or swings the layers apart changes the count. So your guess should specify *how* you fold the second time, not just how many.\n\n```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=paper-fold-layers\n\nroot = LearningSurface([check], \"Paper fold: predict the layer count\", \"Commit to a number before we check it.\", \"ephemeral\")\ncheck = Question([{ header: \"Your guess\", question: \"You fold a paper strip in half once, then in half again. How many layers of paper does the final stack have?\", type: \"choice\", choices: [\"2 layers\", \"4 layers\", \"8 layers\", \"It depends on how the second fold is made\", \"16 layers\"], allowText: true, hint: \"Consider what happens to the thickness at each fold, and whether the second fold stacks the two halves together or folds them apart.\" }], \"ephemeral\", \"Folding and layers\", \"Pick the closest answer, or write your own reasoning.\")\n```\n\nTake your time — and if you say \"it depends,\" tell me *on what*."
        }
